                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS El Paso Tx/Santa Teresa NM",
                "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued October 29 at 1:43PM MDT until October 29 at 2:30PM MDT by NWS El Paso Tx/Santa Teresa NM",
                "description": "FFWEPZ\n\nThe National Weather Service in El Paso Tx/Santa Teresa has issued a\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nThe Salt Burn Scar in...\nNorthern Otero County in south central New Mexico...\n\n* Until 230 PM MDT.\n\n* At 143 PM MDT, Doppler radar indicated thunderstorms producing\nheavy rain over the Salt Burn Scar. Between 1 and 1.5 inches of\nrain have fallen. Additional rainfall amounts up to 1 inch are\npossible in the warned area. Flash flooding is ongoing or expected\nto begin shortly. Excessive rainfall over the burn scar will result\nin debris flow. The debris flow can consist of rock, mud,\nvegetation and other loose materials.\n\nHAZARD...Flash flooding caused by thunderstorms in and around the\nSalt Burn Scar.\n\nSOURCE...Radar indicated.\n\nIMPACT...Flooding of areas in and around the Salt Burn Scar.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nLake Mescalero, Snow Canyon, Bear Canyon, Fence Canyon and Portions\nof US Highway 70 between Apache Summit and the Otero-Lincoln County\nLine.",
                "instruction": "This is a life threatening situation. Heavy rainfall will cause\nextensive and severe flash flooding of creeks...streams...and ditches\nin the Salt Burn Scar. Severe debris flows can also be anticipated\nacross roads. Roads and driveways may be washed away in places. If\nyou encounter flood waters...climb to safety.\n\nTurn around, don't drown when encountering flooded roads. Most flood\ndeaths occur in vehicles.",
                "response": "Avoid",
                "note": null,
